[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 0:20] Okay. So the headline is margins collapsing from 24% to 6% while revenue actually grew 15% — that tells me this is very likely a cost problem rather than a demand problem, though I want to confirm revenue quality.

I'd structure it in three buckets. First, revenue: is the 15% growth coming from new stores, more transactions per store, or higher prices? Because growth from opening stores can carry a lot of fixed cost with it.

Second, costs, split into COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — and operating costs like labor, rent, utilities, marketing, and corporate overhead. I'd want to see each as a percentage of revenue two years ago versus today, so I can isolate which line moved.

Third, anything below the line — interest from debt if they financed expansion, or one-time charges.

Can I start by asking whether the revenue growth is same-store or from new locations?
