[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:00] Okay. Beyond what we've discussed so far, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse this decline? Give me as broad a set of ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:07] Let me spread this out across levers.

On price: the obvious one is raising menu prices. If the basket is up ~38% and they've passed through zero, even a 10–12% price increase recovers a large chunk. They could also do it selectively — raise on the lowest-elasticity items, espresso drinks and food, rather than across the board.

On mix: push customers toward higher-margin items. Food attach, larger sizes, non-dairy upcharges, cold brew, which has lower per-cup bean cost relative to price. Loyalty programs to raise ticket size.

On input cost: lock in forward contracts or hedges on green coffee instead of buying spot, renegotiate dairy and packaging with volume commitments, consolidate suppliers across the 200 stores, reduce portion waste and spoilage.

On product: reformulate slightly — adjust dose per shot, cup sizing, cheaper packaging spec.

On overhead: that 2-point creep is small but real — corporate headcount, marketing spend efficiency.

And longer term, vertical integration on roasting if they aren't already roasting in-house.

Do we have any sense of price elasticity or competitor pricing?
