[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:09] Okay, a broad set of levers across price, mix, input cost, product and overhead. I don't have price elasticity and competitor pricing. The CEO walks in and asks what one thing you'd do first, and what it would take. What's your recommendation?

[candidate · 1:17] Raise menu prices. That's the one thing.

Why: the entire margin decline sits in COGS, which went from 42% to 56% of revenue, and the cause is that input costs rose roughly 38% while menu prices haven't moved in two years. They've been absorbing every cent of inflation. Nothing else closes a 14-point gap fast enough — supplier renegotiation and waste reduction help at the margin but take quarters to land.

Sizing it: to recover 14 points on a 56% COGS base, a price increase of roughly 12–14% holds COGS flat in dollars while lifting revenue, and would bring margin back toward the high teens. And demand looks resilient — volume grew about 19% over the period with no price support, which suggests they have room.

What it takes: a pilot in 20 stores across a few markets, measure transaction counts for 6–8 weeks, then roll out. Risk is elasticity, which we haven't measured — specialty coffee is discretionary, so I'd watch volume closely and pair it with loyalty offers to soften the optics.
