[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:19] Okay. You said volume grew about 19% with no price support, so demand has room. Walk me through how you got that figure, and then tell me the single biggest risk that could make this price increase fail.

[candidate · 1:27] Sure — it falls straight out of the revenue number. Revenue grew about 19% over the two years, and menu prices haven't changed at all, and the store count is fixed at 200. So with price constant and footprint constant, every dollar of revenue growth has to come from units sold. Revenue growth equals volume growth, roughly 19%, and since it's the same 200 stores it's all same-store volume, which is the more encouraging read — they're genuinely selling more cups per location, not just adding doors.

Biggest risk: that the volume growth isn't demand strength, it's price-seeking behavior. If customers have been drifting to Brew & Bean precisely because competitors raised prices and Brew & Bean didn't, then they've effectively been buying share with margin — and a 12–14% increase hands that traffic right back. That would show up as volume falling more than proportionally, wiping out the revenue gain.

That's exactly what the pilot needs to test: not just whether volume dips, but whether it dips hardest in markets where competitors are already priced above us.
